Build a multiple sequence alignment from the \code{refseq} slot of a
\code{\link[phyloseq]{phyloseq-class}} object, or from a set of sequences
given directly. Two backends are available:
\itemize{
\item \code{"decipher"} (default), the pure-R \code{\link[DECIPHER:AlignSeqs]{DECIPHER::AlignSeqs()}}, which needs no
external program;
\item \code{"mafft"}, the MAFFT command-line aligner driven through \code{\link[ips:mafft]{ips::mafft()}},
which is considerably faster on large sets of sequences and is the usual
choice for metabarcoding-scale data.
}

The alignment is returned as a \code{DNAStringSet}, \strong{not} as a phyloseq object.
Gap characters are valid DNA letters, so an alignment can technically be
written back into a \code{refseq} slot, but the sequences of that slot are
consumed as unaligned elsewhere in the pqverse (clustering, BLAST, primer
trimming), so MiscMetabar never does it for you. Feed the returned alignment
to \code{\link[=build_phytree_pq]{build_phytree_pq()}} or \code{\link[Biostrings:writeXStringSet]{Biostrings::writeXStringSet()}} instead.
}
\section{Installing MAFFT}{

MAFFT is an external program and is not installed by MiscMetabar. It is
available from \url{https://mafft.cbrc.jp/alignment/software/}, and from the
usual package managers:
\itemize{
\item Debian / Ubuntu: \verb{sudo apt install mafft}
\item macOS (Homebrew): \verb{brew install mafft}
\item conda: \verb{conda install -c bioconda mafft}
\item Windows: use the installer from the MAFFT website, or run it under WSL.
}

If the executable is not on the \code{PATH}, either pass its path with \code{exec} or
set it once for the session with
\code{options(MiscMetabar.mafftpath = "/path/to/mafft")}.
}

